WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, Hank, it's Monday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.620
I woke up early on Queen's Day here in Amsterdam,
because Henry was all like, "I don't always

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.410
wake up at 5:42 in the morning, but when I
do, I expect to be fed." The Yeti and I dressed

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.879
him in a Dirk Kuyt jersey and headed down
to the Voldelpark that morning. The streets

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:17.690
of Amsterdam were abandoned and only ducks
were on the canals although the port-a-potties

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.039
in the distance hinted that a party was brewing.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:24.390
As we walked we passed this Emily Dickinson
poem "To make a prairie it takes a clover

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:30.980
and one bee, / One clover, and a bee. / And
revery. / The revery alone will do, / If bees

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.980
are few."

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.330
When we got to the Vondelpark, families were
already setting up their stations for the

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:38.480
freemarket, which is so called because none
of the items in it are free, not even the

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.610
back issues of Donald Duck comics.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.660
Queen's Day is a celebration of Queen Beatrix's
mother's birthday, and it involves this strange

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:50.330
city-wide garage sale where people sell artwork,
and old clothes, and unicycle with hula hoops

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:56.020
for tips, and offer carnival games, and oh
my God to be kid on a lake in a bubble. It

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.820
was pretty great, but Henry just wanted to
play in the sandbox. He was like, "Guys, look

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.740
at this stuff! It is AMAZING."

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.140
After a while we walked back to the apartment,
and I watched thousands of people streaming

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.551
downtown. And then I met up with nerdfighter
Tobias who was the only person in all of Amsterdam

00:01:09.551 --> 00:01:10.551
not wearing orange.

00:01:10.551 --> 00:01:12.610
Tobias: I looked in my wardrobe and I didn't
have anything orange.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
John: You're embarrassing yourself.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.960
John: I'm starting to feel a little anxious.
Tobias: Why?

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.300
John: Just because of the number of people
who are here and also wearing orange, which

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.010
is very anxious color for me.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.500
There were no more ducks in the canal and
no more empty streets, just a lot of people

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.850
and free inflatable hats.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.821
John: Now that we've got these hats, it's
definitely a party.

00:01:29.821 --> 00:01:30.821
Tobias: Yeah, it is.

00:01:30.821 --> 00:01:33.790
We finally made it down to the museumplein
where a hundred thousand drunk people were

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.110
listening to music, whereupon Tobias tried
to teach me how to say "Queen's Day" in Dutch.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.170
John: CO-nin-nin-nin-dag
Tobias: Koninginnedag

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.229
John: Ko-nininachdach
Tobias: Koninginnedag

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.479
John: Ko-ni--
Photobomber: Koninginnedag WAHHHHHGH!

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:49.300
We got photobombed quite a lot down there
in the museumplein.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.940
On the walk home Tobias and I had an impassioned
discussion in RE: the providence of this liquid,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.850
and then he bought a biography of Elton John
at a free market.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.120
John: We just saw a guy trading underwear
for Rice Krispie treats. Which is the kind

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.360
of thing that, like, does happen, but not
at 2:20 in the afternoon.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.890
Finally, after an hour of pushing through
crowds I made it home. Techno was rattling

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:17.350
our apartment, but I was happy to live in
a world that finds cause to celebrate. And

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:23.150
happier still that, by law, the party ended
at 9 p.m. That's just good government regulation

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.320
at work, Hank. I'll see you on Wednesday.

